---
functor: 1
title: Grids, matrices, vectors, cases
---

set heading(numbering: none)

A matrix, a vector and cases are grids in a fence. In a grid, a comma ends a cell and a semicolon ends a row. New lines are free space.

| Write | Result |
|:--|:-:|
| `mat(1, 0; 0, 1)` | $mat(1, 0; 0, 1)$ |
| `vec(1, 2, 3)` | $vec(1, 2, 3)$ |
| `cases(x, x ge 0; -x, x lt 0)` | $cases(x, x ge 0; -x, x lt 0)$ |
| `binom(n, k)` | $binom(n, k)$ |
| `grid(a, b; c, d)` | $grid(a, b; c, d)$ |

The four layers:

| Layer | For a matrix |
|:--|:--|
| Meaning | `mat(1, 0; 0, 1)` says "this is a matrix" |
| Style | `set mat(fence: brack)` sets the look of all matrices |
| Construction | a `grid` in a fence |
| Layout | cells in columns, a fence that grows with the content |

# grid

`grid(a, b; c, d)` is the layout with no meaning and no fence. Prefer `mat`, `vec` or `cases`, because a rule can change them. A cell can be empty: `grid(a, , c; d, e, f)`. Rows with different numbers of cells give a warning.

# mat

```fn
set mat(fence: brack)

$$
A = mat(
  a_11, a_12;
  a_21, a_22
)
$$
```

| Rule | Values | Default |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `set mat(fence: ...)` | a fence | `paren` |
| `set mat(grow: ...)` | `stretch`, `magnify` | `stretch` |
| `set mat(inline: ...)` | `small`, `full` | `small` |

In running text a matrix is small by default, as $mat(a, b; c, d)$ here. See [Diagrams](diagrams.html) for diagrams, which are grids with arrows.

A determinant is a matrix with bars: `set mat(fence: vbar)` for all matrices, or `(grid(a, b; c, d)).vbar` for one.

# vec

`vec(x)` is a named vector. `vec(1, 2, 3)` is a vector with components. Both are the same kind, so one rule changes both.

```fn
set vec(style: bold, fence: angle, body: row)

$vec(v) = vec(1, 2, 3)$
```

| Rule | Values | Default |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `set vec(style: ...)` | `arrow`, `bold` | `arrow` |
| `set vec(fence: ...)` | a fence | `paren` |
| `set vec(body: ...)` | `column`, `row` | `column` |
| `set vec(grow: ...)` | `stretch`, `magnify` | `stretch` |
| `set vec(inline: ...)` | `small`, `full` | `small` |

# cases

Each row is a value and a condition.

```fn
set cases(word: "if")

$$
abs(x) = cases(x, x ge 0; -x, x lt 0)
$$
```

| Rule | Values | Default |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `set cases(fence: ...)` | a fence | `brace.left` |
| `set cases(word: ...)` | text before each condition | none |
| `set cases(grow: ...)` | `stretch`, `magnify` | `stretch` |

# binom

`binom(n, k)`. Rules: `set binom(fence: ...)`, `set binom(grow: ...)`.
